    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old female named Catherine with suspected heart failure has a BNP level of 180 pg/mL. Her LVEF is 68%. A colleague states that the BNP is ''not high enough'' to diagnose heart failure. Which of the following is the most accurate interpretation?', '["Obesity does not affect BNP levels, and the standard cutoffs apply universally regardless of body mass index", "BNP is only elevated in HFrEF and has no diagnostic utility in HFpEF; the test should not be ordered if LVEF is >= 50%", "BNP levels are always higher in HFpEF than in HFrEF because diastolic dysfunction generates more myocardial wall stress than systolic dysfunction", "A BNP below 500 pg/mL always excludes heart failure regardless of the clinical presentation, ejection fraction, or echocardiographic findings", "Natriuretic peptide levels in HFpEF are often lower than in HFrEF because the LV is smaller (concentric hypertrophy) and wall stress is less than in a dilated, volume-overloaded ventricle; a BNP > 35 pg/mL or NT-proBNP > 125 pg/mL still supports the diagnosis in the correct clinical context, especially when combined with echocardiographic evidence of diastolic dysfunction"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Natriuretic peptide levels in HFpEF are characteristically LOWER than in HFrEF.** Reasons: (1) BNP/NT-proBNP secretion is proportional to **myocardial wall stress** (LaPlace''s law: wall stress = [pressure × radius] / [2 × wall thickness]). In HFpEF, the LV has **concentric hypertrophy** (thick walls, small cavity), so wall stress is relatively low despite high filling pressures. In HFrEF, the LV is dilated (large radius, thin walls), generating much higher wall stress. (2) **Obesity** (common in HFpEF) independently suppresses BNP levels (adipose tissue expresses neprilysin, which degrades BNP). Therefore, **normal or mildly elevated BNP does NOT exclude HFpEF**, and clinicians must integrate clinical presentation, echocardiography, and biomarkers. (Ref: 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; Maisel AS et al., JACC 2017)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old female named Clara with suspected heart failure has a BNP level of 350 pg/mL. Her LVEF is 58%. A colleague states that the BNP is ''not high enough'' to diagnose heart failure. Which of the following is the most accurate interpretation?', '["BNP is only elevated in HFrEF and has no diagnostic utility in HFpEF; the test should not be ordered if LVEF is >= 50%", "BNP levels are always higher in HFpEF than in HFrEF because diastolic dysfunction generates more myocardial wall stress than systolic dysfunction", "Natriuretic peptide levels in HFpEF are often lower than in HFrEF because the LV is smaller (concentric hypertrophy) and wall stress is less than in a dilated, volume-overloaded ventricle; a BNP > 35 pg/mL or NT-proBNP > 125 pg/mL still supports the diagnosis in the correct clinical context, especially when combined with echocardiographic evidence of diastolic dysfunction", "Obesity does not affect BNP levels, and the standard cutoffs apply universally regardless of body mass index", "A BNP below 500 pg/mL always excludes heart failure regardless of the clinical presentation, ejection fraction, or echocardiographic findings"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Natriuretic peptide levels in HFpEF are characteristically LOWER than in HFrEF.** Reasons: (1) BNP/NT-proBNP secretion is proportional to **myocardial wall stress** (LaPlace''s law: wall stress = [pressure × radius] / [2 × wall thickness]). In HFpEF, the LV has **concentric hypertrophy** (thick walls, small cavity), so wall stress is relatively low despite high filling pressures. In HFrEF, the LV is dilated (large radius, thin walls), generating much higher wall stress. (2) **Obesity** (common in HFpEF) independently suppresses BNP levels (adipose tissue expresses neprilysin, which degrades BNP). Therefore, **normal or mildly elevated BNP does NOT exclude HFpEF**, and clinicians must integrate clinical presentation, echocardiography, and biomarkers. (Ref: 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; Maisel AS et al., JACC 2017)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 96-year-old female named Alice with suspected heart failure has a BNP level of 320 pg/mL. Her LVEF is 68%. A colleague states that the BNP is ''not high enough'' to diagnose heart failure. Which of the following is the most accurate interpretation?', '["BNP levels are always higher in HFpEF than in HFrEF because diastolic dysfunction generates more myocardial wall stress than systolic dysfunction", "Obesity does not affect BNP levels, and the standard cutoffs apply universally regardless of body mass index", "A BNP below 500 pg/mL always excludes heart failure regardless of the clinical presentation, ejection fraction, or echocardiographic findings", "BNP is only elevated in HFrEF and has no diagnostic utility in HFpEF; the test should not be ordered if LVEF is >= 50%", "Natriuretic peptide levels in HFpEF are often lower than in HFrEF because the LV is smaller (concentric hypertrophy) and wall stress is less than in a dilated, volume-overloaded ventricle; a BNP > 35 pg/mL or NT-proBNP > 125 pg/mL still supports the diagnosis in the correct clinical context, especially when combined with echocardiographic evidence of diastolic dysfunction"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Natriuretic peptide levels in HFpEF are characteristically LOWER than in HFrEF.** Reasons: (1) BNP/NT-proBNP secretion is proportional to **myocardial wall stress** (LaPlace''s law: wall stress = [pressure × radius] / [2 × wall thickness]). In HFpEF, the LV has **concentric hypertrophy** (thick walls, small cavity), so wall stress is relatively low despite high filling pressures. In HFrEF, the LV is dilated (large radius, thin walls), generating much higher wall stress. (2) **Obesity** (common in HFpEF) independently suppresses BNP levels (adipose tissue expresses neprilysin, which degrades BNP). Therefore, **normal or mildly elevated BNP does NOT exclude HFpEF**, and clinicians must integrate clinical presentation, echocardiography, and biomarkers. (Ref: 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; Maisel AS et al., JACC 2017)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old female named Marie with exertional dyspnea and preserved LVEF (60%) undergoes a comprehensive echocardiogram. Which of the following echocardiographic parameters is most useful for estimating left ventricular filling pressures and supporting a diagnosis of HFpEF?', '["The E/e'' ratio (ratio of mitral inflow E-wave velocity to tissue Doppler e'' velocity at the mitral annulus); an E/e'' ratio >= 14 suggests elevated LV filling pressures, and combined with enlarged left atrial volume index (> 34 mL/m2) and elevated tricuspid regurgitation velocity (> 2.8 m/s), strongly supports diastolic dysfunction", "The inferior vena cava (IVC) diameter alone; an IVC < 2.1 cm with respiratory variation definitively excludes HFpEF", "The right ventricular systolic pressure estimated by tricuspid regurgitation velocity; this is only relevant for diagnosing pulmonary embolism", "The left ventricular internal diameter in diastole (LVIDd); a normal LVIDd confirms normal diastolic function regardless of filling pressures", "The aortic valve area calculated by the continuity equation; aortic stenosis is the sole cause of diastolic dysfunction in older adults"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **E/e'' ratio** is the single most important non-invasive echocardiographic parameter for estimating **LV filling pressures** in suspected HFpEF. **E wave**: early diastolic mitral inflow velocity (reflects the pressure gradient between LA and LV). **e'' velocity**: early diastolic mitral annular velocity on tissue Doppler (reflects myocardial relaxation). Interpretation: (1) **E/e'' < 8**: normal LV filling pressures, (2) **E/e'' 8-14**: indeterminate (need additional criteria), (3) **E/e'' >= 14**: elevated LV filling pressures (suggests diastolic dysfunction). Additional supporting criteria: **Enlarged LA volume index > 34 mL/m2** (reflects chronic elevation of LA pressure), **Tricuspid regurgitation velocity > 2.8 m/s** (reflects elevated pulmonary artery systolic pressure). (Ref: ASE/EACVI 2016 Guidelines; 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA; Hazzard''s Ch. 38)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old male named Donald with exertional dyspnea and preserved LVEF (55%) undergoes a comprehensive echocardiogram. Which of the following echocardiographic parameters is most useful for estimating left ventricular filling pressures and supporting a diagnosis of HFpEF?', '["The left ventricular internal diameter in diastole (LVIDd); a normal LVIDd confirms normal diastolic function regardless of filling pressures", "The aortic valve area calculated by the continuity equation; aortic stenosis is the sole cause of diastolic dysfunction in older adults", "The E/e'' ratio (ratio of mitral inflow E-wave velocity to tissue Doppler e'' velocity at the mitral annulus); an E/e'' ratio >= 14 suggests elevated LV filling pressures, and combined with enlarged left atrial volume index (> 34 mL/m2) and elevated tricuspid regurgitation velocity (> 2.8 m/s), strongly supports diastolic dysfunction", "The right ventricular systolic pressure estimated by tricuspid regurgitation velocity; this is only relevant for diagnosing pulmonary embolism", "The inferior vena cava (IVC) diameter alone; an IVC < 2.1 cm with respiratory variation definitively excludes HFpEF"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **E/e'' ratio** is the single most important non-invasive echocardiographic parameter for estimating **LV filling pressures** in suspected HFpEF. **E wave**: early diastolic mitral inflow velocity (reflects the pressure gradient between LA and LV). **e'' velocity**: early diastolic mitral annular velocity on tissue Doppler (reflects myocardial relaxation). Interpretation: (1) **E/e'' < 8**: normal LV filling pressures, (2) **E/e'' 8-14**: indeterminate (need additional criteria), (3) **E/e'' >= 14**: elevated LV filling pressures (suggests diastolic dysfunction). Additional supporting criteria: **Enlarged LA volume index > 34 mL/m2** (reflects chronic elevation of LA pressure), **Tricuspid regurgitation velocity > 2.8 m/s** (reflects elevated pulmonary artery systolic pressure). (Ref: ASE/EACVI 2016 Guidelines; 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA; Hazzard''s Ch. 38)'),
