    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old male named Roy with exertional dyspnea and preserved LVEF (70%) undergoes a comprehensive echocardiogram. Which of the following echocardiographic parameters is most useful for estimating left ventricular filling pressures and supporting a diagnosis of HFpEF?', '["The E/e'' ratio (ratio of mitral inflow E-wave velocity to tissue Doppler e'' velocity at the mitral annulus); an E/e'' ratio >= 14 suggests elevated LV filling pressures, and combined with enlarged left atrial volume index (> 34 mL/m2) and elevated tricuspid regurgitation velocity (> 2.8 m/s), strongly supports diastolic dysfunction", "The aortic valve area calculated by the continuity equation; aortic stenosis is the sole cause of diastolic dysfunction in older adults", "The left ventricular internal diameter in diastole (LVIDd); a normal LVIDd confirms normal diastolic function regardless of filling pressures", "The right ventricular systolic pressure estimated by tricuspid regurgitation velocity; this is only relevant for diagnosing pulmonary embolism", "The inferior vena cava (IVC) diameter alone; an IVC < 2.1 cm with respiratory variation definitively excludes HFpEF"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **E/e'' ratio** is the single most important non-invasive echocardiographic parameter for estimating **LV filling pressures** in suspected HFpEF. **E wave**: early diastolic mitral inflow velocity (reflects the pressure gradient between LA and LV). **e'' velocity**: early diastolic mitral annular velocity on tissue Doppler (reflects myocardial relaxation). Interpretation: (1) **E/e'' < 8**: normal LV filling pressures, (2) **E/e'' 8-14**: indeterminate (need additional criteria), (3) **E/e'' >= 14**: elevated LV filling pressures (suggests diastolic dysfunction). Additional supporting criteria: **Enlarged LA volume index > 34 mL/m2** (reflects chronic elevation of LA pressure), **Tricuspid regurgitation velocity > 2.8 m/s** (reflects elevated pulmonary artery systolic pressure). (Ref: ASE