    (v_topic_id, 'A 96-year-old female named Grace with exertional dyspnea and preserved LVEF (60%) undergoes a comprehensive echocardiogram. Which of the following echocardiographic parameters is most useful for estimating left ventricular filling pressures and supporting a diagnosis of HFpEF?', '["The aortic valve area calculated by the continuity equation; aortic stenosis is the sole cause of diastolic dysfunction in older adults", "The inferior vena cava (IVC) diameter alone; an IVC < 2.1 cm with respiratory variation definitively excludes HFpEF", "The right ventricular systolic pressure estimated by tricuspid regurgitation velocity; this is only relevant for diagnosing pulmonary embolism", "The left ventricular internal diameter in diastole (LVIDd); a normal LVIDd confirms normal diastolic function regardless of filling pressures", "The E/e'' ratio (ratio of mitral inflow E-wave velocity to tissue Doppler e'' velocity at the mitral annulus); an E/e'' ratio >= 14 suggests elevated LV filling pressures, and combined with enlarged left atrial volume index (> 34 mL/m2) and elevated tricuspid regurgitation velocity (> 2.8 m/s), strongly supports diastolic dysfunction"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **E/e'' ratio** is the single most important non-invasive echocardiographic parameter for estimating **LV filling pressures** in suspected HFpEF. **E wave**: early diastolic mitral inflow velocity (reflects the pressure gradient between LA and LV). **e'' velocity**: early diastolic mitral annular velocity on tissue Doppler (reflects myocardial relaxation). Interpretation: (1) **E/e'' < 8**: normal LV filling pressures, (2) **E/e'' 8-14**: indeterminate (need additional criteria), (3) **E/e'' >= 14**: elevated LV filling pressures (suggests diastolic dysfunction). Additional supporting criteria: **Enlarged LA volume index > 34 mL/m2** (reflects chronic elevation of LA pressure), **Tricuspid regurgitation velocity > 2.8 m/s** (reflects elevated pulmonary artery systolic pressure). (Ref: ASE/EACVI 2016 Guidelines; 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA; Hazzard''s Ch. 38)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old female named Clara with HFpEF (LVEF 62%) presents with worsening dyspnea, orthopnea, bilateral lower-extremity edema, and weight gain of 6 lbs over the past week. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial pharmacological management of the acute congestion?', '["Intravenous or oral loop diuretics (e.g., bumetanide) are the cornerstone of congestion management in HFpEF; they reduce preload by promoting sodium and water excretion, alleviating pulmonary and peripheral edema; however, they must be titrated carefully to avoid excessive volume depletion, hypotension, and prerenal azotemia", "Administer intravenous dobutamine to increase cardiac contractility and improve ejection fraction", "Prescribe a high-sodium diet (> 6 g/day) to increase intravascular volume and improve cardiac output", "Start high-dose beta-blocker therapy (metoprolol 200 mg daily) to reduce heart rate and improve diastolic filling time as the primary treatment for acute congestion", "Initiate continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) as the sole therapy for HFpEF-related congestion without any diuretics"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Loop diuretics** are the **mainstay of congestion management** in ALL forms of heart failure, including HFpEF. In HFpEF, the primary symptom is **volume overload** (dyspnea, edema, weight gain) due to elevated LV filling pressures. Diuretics reduce **preload** by promoting renal sodium and water excretion, thereby: (1) Reducing pulmonary venous pressure (relieving dyspnea and orthopnea), (2) Reducing peripheral edema, (3) Reducing left atrial pressure (improving diastolic filling). **Important caveats in HFpEF**: These patients are highly **preload-sensitive** — the stiff, non-compliant ventricle depends on adequate filling pressure to maintain stroke volume. Over-diuresis can cause: (1) **Hypotension**, (2) **Prerenal AKI**, (3) **Reduced cardiac output**. Therefore, diuretics must be carefully titrated to the **lowest effective dose**. (Ref: 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old female named Dorothy with HFpEF (LVEF 70%) presents with worsening dyspnea, orthopnea, bilateral lower-extremity edema, and weight gain of 5 lbs over the past week. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial pharmacological management of the acute congestion?', '["Initiate continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) as the sole therapy for HFpEF-related congestion without any diuretics", "Prescribe a high-sodium diet (> 6 g/day) to increase intravascular volume and improve cardiac output", "Intravenous or oral loop diuretics (e.g., torsemide) are the cornerstone of congestion management in HFpEF; they reduce preload by promoting sodium and water excretion, alleviating pulmonary and peripheral edema; however, they must be titrated carefully to avoid excessive volume depletion, hypotension, and prerenal azotemia", "Start high-dose beta-blocker therapy (metoprolol 200 mg daily) to reduce heart rate and improve diastolic filling time as the primary treatment for acute congestion", "Administer intravenous dobutamine to increase cardiac contractility and improve ejection fraction"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Loop diuretics** are the **mainstay of congestion management** in ALL forms of heart failure, including HFpEF. In HFpEF, the primary symptom is **volume overload** (dyspnea, edema, weight gain) due to elevated LV filling pressures. Diuretics reduce **preload** by promoting renal sodium and water excretion, thereby: (1) Reducing pulmonary venous pressure (relieving dyspnea and orthopnea), (2) Reducing peripheral edema, (3) Reducing left atrial pressure (improving diastolic filling). **Important caveats in HFpEF**: These patients are highly **preload-sensitive** — the stiff, non-compliant ventricle depends on adequate filling pressure to maintain stroke volume. Over-diuresis can cause: (1) **Hypotension**, (2) **Prerenal AKI**, (3) **Reduced cardiac output**. Therefore, diuretics must be carefully titrated to the **lowest effective dose**.