    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old female named Edna with HFpEF (LVEF 55%) presents with worsening dyspnea, orthopnea, bilateral lower-extremity edema, and weight gain of 4 lbs over the past week. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial pharmacological management of the acute congestion?', '["Initiate continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) as the sole therapy for HFpEF-related congestion without any diuretics", "Prescribe a high-sodium diet (> 6 g/day) to increase intravascular volume and improve cardiac output", "Administer intravenous dobutamine to increase cardiac contractility and improve ejection fraction", "Start high-dose beta-blocker therapy (metoprolol 200 mg daily) to reduce heart rate and improve diastolic filling time as the primary treatment for acute congestion", "Intravenous or oral loop diuretics (e.g., bumetanide) are the cornerstone of congestion management in HFpEF; they reduce preload by promoting sodium and water excretion, alleviating pulmonary and peripheral edema; however, they must be titrated carefully to avoid excessive volume depletion, hypotension, and prerenal azotemia"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Loop diuretics** are the **mainstay of congestion management** in ALL forms of heart failure, including HFpEF. In HFpEF, the primary symptom is **volume overload** (dyspnea, edema, weight gain) due to elevated LV filling pressures. Diuretics reduce **preload** by promoting renal sodium and water excretion, thereby: (1) Reducing pulmonary venous pressure (relieving dyspnea and orthopnea), (2) Reducing peripheral edema, (3) Reducing left atrial pressure (improving diastolic filling). **Important caveats in HFpEF**: These patients are highly **preload-sensitive** — the stiff, non-compliant ventricle depends on adequate filling pressure to maintain stroke volume. Over-diuresis can cause: (1) **Hypotension**, (2) **Prerenal AKI**, (3) **Reduced cardiac output**. Therefore, diuretics must be carefully titrated to the **lowest effective dose**. (Ref: 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 96-year-old male named William with HFpEF (LVEF 58%) is being optimized on diuretics but continues to have recurrent hospitalizations. His physician adds dapagliflozin to the regimen. Which of the following best describes the evidence supporting SGLT2 inhibitors in HFpEF?', '["The PARAGON-HF trial proved that SGLT2 inhibitors are harmful in HFpEF and increase all-cause mortality", "The EMPEROR-Preserved trial (empagliflozin) and the DELIVER trial (dapagliflozin) both demonstrated significant reductions in the composite endpoint of cardiovascular death or heart failure hospitalization in patients with HFpEF, making SGLT2 inhibitors the first drug class with robust evidence across the full spectrum of LVEF", "SGLT2 inhibitors improve ejection fraction from 60% to 80%, converting HFpEF into a hyperdynamic state", "SGLT2 inhibitors are contraindicated in HFpEF because they cause excessive dehydration and hypotension in patients with diastolic dysfunction", "SGLT2 inhibitors only benefit patients with type 2 diabetes and have no cardiovascular effects in non-diabetic HFpEF patients"]'::jsonb, 1, '**SGLT2 inhibitors** are now the **first and only drug class with robust clinical trial evidence** for reducing heart failure outcomes in HFpEF. Key trials: (1) **EMPEROR-Preserved (empagliflozin, NEJM 2021)**: Reduced the composite of cardiovascular death or HF hospitalization by **21%** in patients with LVEF > 40% (including HFpEF), regardless of diabetes status. (2) **DELIVER (dapagliflozin, NEJM 2022)**: Reduced the same composite endpoint by **18%** in HFpEF (LVEF > 40%), also regardless of diabetes. These results, combined with prior HFrEF data (DAPA-HF, EMPEROR-Reduced), establish SGLT2 inhibitors as beneficial **across the entire LVEF spectrum**. Mechanisms: osmotic diuresis, natriuresis, reduced preload, improved myocardial energetics (ketone body utilization), anti-fibrotic effects. (Ref: EMPEROR-Preserved, NEJM 2021; DELIVER, NEJM 2022; 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guidelines)'),
