    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old male named Paul with HFpEF (LVEF 65%) is being optimized on diuretics but continues to have recurrent hospitalizations. His physician adds empagliflozin to the regimen. Which of the following best describes the evidence supporting SGLT2 inhibitors in HFpEF?', '["The EMPEROR-Preserved trial (empagliflozin) and the DELIVER trial (dapagliflozin) both demonstrated significant reductions in the composite endpoint of cardiovascular death or heart failure hospitalization in patients with HFpEF, making SGLT2 inhibitors the first drug class with robust evidence across the full spectrum of LVEF", "The PARAGON-HF trial proved that SGLT2 inhibitors are harmful in HFpEF and increase all-cause mortality", "SGLT2 inhibitors improve ejection fraction from 60% to 80%, converting HFpEF into a hyperdynamic state", "SGLT2 inhibitors only benefit patients with type 2 diabetes and have no cardiovascular effects in non-diabetic HFpEF patients", "SGLT2 inhibitors are contraindicated in HFpEF because they cause excessive dehydration and hypotension in patients with diastolic dysfunction"]'::jsonb, 0, '**SGLT2 inhibitors** are now the **first and only drug class with robust clinical trial evidence** for reducing heart failure outcomes in HFpEF. Key trials: (1) **EMPEROR-Preserved (empagliflozin, NEJM 2021)**: Reduced the composite of cardiovascular death or HF hospitalization by **21%** in patients with LVEF > 40% (including HFpEF), regardless of diabetes status. (2) **DELIVER (dapagliflozin, NEJM 2022)**: Reduced the same composite endpoint by **18%** in HFpEF (LVEF > 40%), also regardless of diabetes. These results, combined with prior HFrEF data (DAPA-HF, EMPEROR-Reduced), establish SGLT2 inhibitors as beneficial **across the entire LVEF spectrum**. Mechanisms: osmotic diuresis, natriuresis, reduced preload, improved myocardial energetics (ketone body utilization), anti-fibrotic effects. (Ref: EMPEROR-Preserved, NEJM 2021; DELIVER, NEJM 2022; 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old male named Henry with HFpEF (LVEF 58%) is being optimized on diuretics but continues to have recurrent hospitalizations. His physician adds dapagliflozin to the regimen. Which of the following best describes the evidence supporting SGLT2 inhibitors in HFpEF?', '["SGLT2 inhibitors are contraindicated in HFpEF because they cause excessive dehydration and hypotension in patients with diastolic dysfunction", "The EMPEROR-Preserved trial (empagliflozin) and the DELIVER trial (dapagliflozin) both demonstrated significant reductions in the composite endpoint of cardiovascular death or heart failure hospitalization in patients with HFpEF, making SGLT2 inhibitors the first drug class with robust evidence across the full spectrum of LVEF", "The PARAGON-HF trial proved that SGLT2 inhibitors are harmful in HFpEF and increase all-cause mortality", "SGLT2 inhibitors only benefit patients with type 2 diabetes and have no cardiovascular effects in non-diabetic HFpEF patients", "SGLT2 inhibitors improve ejection fraction from 60% to 80%, converting HFpEF into a hyperdynamic state"]'::jsonb, 1, '**SGLT2 inhibitors** are now the **first and only drug class with robust clinical trial evidence** for reducing heart failure outcomes in HFpEF. Key trials: (1) **EMPEROR-Preserved (empagliflozin, NEJM 2021)**: Reduced the composite of cardiovascular death or HF hospitalization by **21%** in patients with LVEF > 40% (including HFpEF), regardless of diabetes status. (2) **DELIVER (dapagliflozin, NEJM 2022)**: Reduced the same composite endpoint by **18%** in HFpEF (LVEF > 40%), also regardless of diabetes. These results, combined with prior HFrEF data (DAPA-HF, EMPEROR-Reduced), establish SGLT2 inhibitors as beneficial **across the entire LVEF spectrum**. Mechanisms: osmotic diuresis, natriuresis, reduced preload, improved myocardial energetics (ketone body utilization), anti-fibrotic effects. (Ref: EMPEROR-Preserved, NEJM 2021; DELIVER, NEJM 2022; 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old male named Edward with HFpEF (LVEF 68%) asks about the role of eplerenone in his condition. Which of the following best summarizes the evidence from the TOPCAT trial regarding spironolactone in HFpEF?', '["The TOPCAT trial conclusively proved that spironolactone reduces all-cause mortality by 50% in HFpEF and is now a Class I recommendation", "The TOPCAT trial showed that spironolactone did not significantly reduce the primary composite endpoint of cardiovascular death, cardiac arrest, or HF hospitalization in the overall HFpEF population; however, regional subgroup analyses (Americas cohort) suggested potential benefit, and spironolactone may reduce HF hospitalizations; the 2022 guidelines give it a Class 2b recommendation for selected HFpEF patients", "Spironolactone is absolutely contraindicated in HFpEF because it worsens diastolic dysfunction by promoting myocardial fibrosis", "The TOPCAT trial demonstrated that eplerenone (not spironolactone) was the only effective MRA in HFpEF", "The TOPCAT trial was never completed and provides no evidence regarding MRA therapy in HFpEF"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **TOPCAT (Treatment of Preserved Cardiac Function Heart Failure with an Aldosterone Antagonist) trial** was a large RCT (3,445 patients) testing **spironolactone vs. placebo** in HFpEF (LVEF >= 45%). Overall results: Spironolactone **did NOT significantly reduce** the primary composite endpoint (CV death, cardiac arrest, or HF hospitalization; HR 0.89, p = 0.14). However: (1) **Regional heterogeneity**: The Americas cohort showed a significant benefit (HR 0.82), while the Russia/Georgia cohort showed no benefit (suspected enrollment and adherence issues). (2) Spironolactone reduced **HF hospitalizations** as a secondary endpoint. (3) The **2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guidelines** give spironolactone a **Class 2b recommendation** (''may be reasonable'') for selected HFpEF patients to reduce hospitalizations. (Ref: TOPCAT Trial, NEJM 2014; 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38)'),
