    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old male named Donald with HFpEF (LVEF 62%) asks about the role of spironolactone in his condition. Which of the following best summarizes the evidence from the TOPCAT trial regarding spironolactone in HFpEF?', '["Spironolactone is absolutely contraindicated in HFpEF because it worsens diastolic dysfunction by promoting myocardial fibrosis", "The TOPCAT trial demonstrated that eplerenone (not spironolactone) was the only effective MRA in HFpEF", "The TOPCAT trial was never completed and provides no evidence regarding MRA therapy in HFpEF", "The TOPCAT trial showed that spironolactone did not significantly reduce the primary composite endpoint of cardiovascular death, cardiac arrest, or HF hospitalization in the overall HFpEF population; however, regional subgroup analyses (Americas cohort) suggested potential benefit, and spironolactone may reduce HF hospitalizations; the 2022 guidelines give it a Class 2b recommendation for selected HFpEF patients", "The TOPCAT trial conclusively proved that spironolactone reduces all-cause mortality by 50% in HFpEF and is now a Class I recommendation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **TOPCAT (Treatment of Preserved Cardiac Function Heart Failure with an Aldosterone Antagonist) trial** was a large RCT (3,445 patients) testing **spironolactone vs. placebo** in HFpEF (LVEF >= 45%). Overall results: Spironolactone **did NOT significantly reduce** the primary composite endpoint (CV death, cardiac arrest, or HF hospitalization; HR 0.89, p = 0.14). However: (1) **Regional heterogeneity**: The Americas cohort showed a significant benefit (HR 0.82), while the Russia/Georgia cohort showed no benefit (suspected enrollment and adherence issues). (2) Spironolactone reduced **HF hospitalizations** as a secondary endpoint. (3) The **2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guidelines** give spironolactone a **Class 2b recommendation** (''may be reasonable'') for selected HFpEF patients to reduce hospitalizations. (Ref: TOPCAT Trial, NEJM 2014; 2022 AHA/A