    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old female named Gladys with HFpEF (LVEF 70%) asks about the role of spironolactone in her condition. Which of the following best summarizes the evidence from the TOPCAT trial regarding spironolactone in HFpEF?', '["The TOPCAT trial conclusively proved that spironolactone reduces all-cause mortality by 50% in HFpEF and is now a Class I recommendation", "Spironolactone is absolutely contraindicated in HFpEF because it worsens diastolic dysfunction by promoting myocardial fibrosis", "The TOPCAT trial showed that spironolactone did not significantly reduce the primary composite endpoint of cardiovascular death, cardiac arrest, or HF hospitalization in the overall HFpEF population; however, regional subgroup analyses (Americas cohort) suggested potential benefit, and spironolactone may reduce HF hospitalizations; the 2022 guidelines give it a Class 2b recommendation for selected HFpEF patients", "The TOPCAT trial demonstrated that eplerenone (not spironolactone) was the only effective MRA in HFpEF", "The TOPCAT trial was never completed and provides no evidence regarding MRA therapy in HFpEF"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **TOPCAT (Treatment of Preserved Cardiac Function Heart Failure with an Aldosterone Antagonist) trial** was a large RCT (3,445 patients) testing **spironolactone vs. placebo** in HFpEF (LVEF >= 45%). Overall results: Spironolactone **did NOT significantly reduce** the primary composite endpoint (CV death, cardiac arrest, or HF hospitalization; HR 0.89, p = 0.14). However: (1) **Regional heterogeneity**: The Americas cohort showed a significant benefit (HR 0.82), while the Russia/Georgia cohort showed no benefit (suspected enrollment and adherence issues). (2) Spironolactone reduced **HF hospitalizations** as a secondary endpoint. (3) The **2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guidelines** give spironolactone a **Class 2b recommendation** (''may be reasonable'') for selected HFpEF patients to reduce hospitalizations. (Ref: TOPCAT Trial, NEJM 2014; 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 90-year-old male named William with HFpEF (LVEF 65%) has been stable on torsemide but presents with acute decompensation (dyspnea at rest, SpO2 91%, bilateral crackles) after a dietary indiscretion (high-sodium restaurant meal). Body weight has increased by 6 lbs from baseline. What is the most appropriate acute management?', '["Start intravenous milrinone to increase myocardial contractility and reduce filling pressures", "Administer intravenous torsemide with continuous monitoring of urine output, daily weights, serum electrolytes, and renal function; target net negative fluid balance while avoiding excessive diuresis that can precipitate hypotension and prerenal azotemia in the preload-sensitive HFpEF ventricle", "Administer intravenous normal saline bolus (2 liters) to improve cardiac output by increasing preload in the stiff ventricle", "Perform emergent pericardiocentesis to relieve the fluid overload", "Administer high-dose intravenous nitroglycerin as the sole therapy, as diuretics are contraindicated in acute HFpEF decompensation"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Acute decompensated HFpEF** management centers on **volume removal with IV loop diuretics** while maintaining vigilant monitoring. Key principles: (1) **IV loop diuretics** (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide) are first-line for acute congestion — they promote rapid natriuresis and diuresis, reducing pulmonary edema and symptom burden. (2) **Monitor closely**: urine output (target > 0.5-1 mL/kg/hr), daily weights (target 0.5-1 kg/day net loss), serum electrolytes (K+, Na+, Mg2+), and renal function (Cr). (3) **Avoid over-diuresis**: HFpEF patients have a **stiff, non-compliant ventricle** that is highly **preload-dependent**. Excessive volume removal drops filling pressures below the steep portion of the Frank-Starling curve, causing **hypotension, low cardiac output, and prerenal AKI**. (Ref: 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female named Helen with HFpEF (LVEF 65%) has been stable on bumetanide but presents with acute decompensation (dyspnea at rest, SpO2 91%, bilateral crackles) after a dietary indiscretion (high-sodium restaurant meal). Body weight has increased by 6 lbs from baseline. What is the most appropriate acute management?', '["Perform emergent pericardiocentesis to relieve the fluid overload", "Administer high-dose intravenous nitroglycerin as the sole therapy, as diuretics are contraindicated in acute HFpEF decompensation", "Administer intravenous bumetanide with continuous monitoring of urine output, daily weights, serum electrolytes, and renal function; target net negative fluid balance while avoiding excessive diuresis that can precipitate hypotension and prerenal azotemia in the preload-sensitive HFpEF ventricle", "Administer intravenous normal saline bolus (2 liters) to improve cardiac output by increasing preload in the stiff ventricle", "Start intravenous milrinone to increase myocardial contractility and reduce filling pressures"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Acute decompensated HFpEF** management centers on **volume removal with IV loop diuretics** while maintaining vigilant monitoring. Key principles: (1) **IV loop diuretics** (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide) are first-line for acute congestion — they promote rapid natriuresis and diuresis, reducing pulmonary edema and symptom burden. (2) **Monitor closely**: urine output (target > 0.5-1 mL/kg/hr), daily weights (target 0.5-1 kg/day net loss), serum electrolytes (K+, Na+, Mg2+), and renal function (Cr). (3) **Avoid over-diuresis**: HFpEF patients have a **stiff, non-compliant ventricle** that is highly **preload-dependent**. Excessive volume removal drops filling pressures below the steep portion of the Frank-Starling curve, causing **hypotension, low cardiac output, and prerenal AKI**. (Ref: 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female named Phyllis with HFpEF (LVEF 60%) has been stable on furosemide but presents with acute decompensation (dyspnea at rest, SpO2 91%, bilateral crackles) after a dietary indiscretion (high-sodium restaurant meal). Body weight has increased by 3 lbs from baseline. What is the most appropriate acute management?', '["Start intravenous milrinone to increase myocardial contractility and reduce filling pressures", "Administer intravenous normal saline bolus (2 liters) to improve cardiac output by increasing preload in the stiff ventricle", "Administer intravenous furosemide with continuous monitoring of urine output, daily weights, serum electrolytes, and renal function; target net negative fluid balance while avoiding excessive diuresis that can precipitate hypotension and prerenal azotemia in the preload-sensitive HFpEF ventricle", "Administer high-dose intravenous nitroglycerin as the sole therapy, as diuretics are contraindicated in acute HFpEF decompensation", "Perform emergent pericardiocentesis to relieve the fluid overload"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Acute decompensated HFpEF** management centers on **volume removal with IV loop diuretics** while maintaining vigilant monitoring. Key principles: (1) **IV loop diuretics** (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide) are first-line for acute congestion — they promote rapid natriuresis and diuresis, reducing pulmonary edema and symptom burden. (2) **Monitor closely**: urine output (target > 0.5-1 mL/kg/hr), daily weights (target 0.5-1 kg/day net loss), serum electrolytes (K+, Na+, Mg2+), and renal function (Cr). (3) **Avoid over-diuresis**: HFpEF patients have a **stiff, non-compliant ventricle** that is highly **preload-dependent**. Excessive volume removal drops filling pressures below the steep portion of the Frank-Starling curve, causing **hypotension, low cardiac output, and prerenal AKI**. (Ref: 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old female named Margaret with HFpEF (LVEF 65%) has been stable on bumetanide but presents with acute decompensation (dyspnea at rest, SpO2 91%, bilateral crackles) after a dietary indiscretion (high-sodium restaurant meal). Body weight has increased by 6 lbs from baseline. What is the most appropriate acute management?', '["Start intravenous milrinone to increase myocardial contractility and reduce filling pressures", "Administer high-dose intravenous nitroglycerin as the sole therapy, as diuretics are contraindicated in acute HFpEF decompensation", "Administer intravenous normal saline bolus (2 liters) to improve cardiac output by increasing preload in the stiff ventricle", "Perform emergent pericardiocentesis to relieve the fluid overload", "Administer intravenous bumetanide with continuous monitoring of urine output, daily weights, serum electrolytes, and renal function; target net negative fluid balance while avoiding excessive diuresis that can precipitate hypotension and prerenal azotemia in the preload-sensitive HFpEF ventricle"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Acute decompensated HFpEF** management centers on **volume removal with IV loop diuretics** while maintaining vigilant monitoring. Key principles: (1) **IV loop diuretics** (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide) are first-line for acute congestion — they promote rapid natriuresis and diuresis, reducing pulmonary edema and symptom burden. (2) **Monitor closely**: urine output (target > 0.5-1 mL/kg/hr), daily weights (target 0.5-1 kg/day net loss), serum electrolytes (K+, Na+, Mg2+), and renal function (Cr). (3) **Avoid over-diuresis**: HFpEF patients have a **stiff, non-compliant ventricle** that is highly **preload-dependent**. Excessive volume removal drops filling pressures below the steep portion of the Frank-Starling curve, causing **hypotension, low cardiac output, and prerenal AKI**. (Ref: 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
