    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old male named Ralph with HFpEF (LVEF 55%) has been stable on torsemide but presents with acute decompensation (dyspnea at rest, SpO2 91%, bilateral crackles) after a dietary indiscretion (high-sodium restaurant meal). Body weight has increased by 5 lbs from baseline. What is the most appropriate acute management?', '["Administer intravenous torsemide with continuous monitoring of urine output, daily weights, serum electrolytes, and renal function; target net negative fluid balance while avoiding excessive diuresis that can precipitate hypotension and prerenal azotemia in the preload-sensitive HFpEF ventricle", "Administer high-dose intravenous nitroglycerin as the sole therapy, as diuretics are contraindicated in acute HFpEF decompensation", "Start intravenous milrinone to increase myocardial contractility and reduce filling pressures", "Administer intravenous normal saline bolus (2 liters) to improve cardiac output by increasing preload in the stiff ventricle", "Perform emergent pericardiocentesis to relieve the fluid overload"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Acute decompensated HFpEF** management centers on **volume removal with IV loop diuretics** while maintaining vigilant monitoring. Key principles: (1) **IV loop diuretics** (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide) are first-line for acute congestion — they promote rapid natriuresis and diuresis, reducing pulmonary edema and symptom burden. (2) **Monitor closely**: urine output (target > 0.5-1 mL/kg/hr), daily weights (target 0.5-1 kg/day net loss), serum electrolytes (K+, Na+, Mg2+), and renal function (Cr). (3) **Avoid over-diuresis**: HFpEF patients have a **stiff, non-compliant ventricle** that is highly **preload-dependent**. Excessive volume removal drops filling pressures below the steep portion of the Frank-Starling curve, causing **hypotension, low cardiac output, and prerenal AKI**. (Ref: 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old male named Thomas with HFpEF (LVEF 58%) has been stable on furosemide but presents with acute decompensation (dyspnea at rest, SpO2 91%, bilateral crackles) after a dietary indiscretion (high-sodium restaurant meal). Body weight has increased by 8 lbs from baseline. What is the most appropriate acute management?', '["Administer high-dose intravenous nitroglycerin as the sole therapy, as diuretics are contraindicated in acute HFpEF decompensation", "Administer intravenous furosemide with continuous monitoring of urine output, daily weights, serum electrolytes, and renal function; target net negative fluid balance while avoiding excessive diuresis that can precipitate hypotension and prerenal azotemia in the preload-sensitive HFpEF ventricle", "Perform emergent pericardiocentesis to relieve the fluid overload", "Start intravenous milrinone to increase myocardial contractility and reduce filling pressures", "Administer intravenous normal saline bolus (2 liters) to improve cardiac output by increasing preload in the stiff ventricle"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Acute decompensated HFpEF** management centers on **volume removal with IV loop diuretics** while maintaining vigilant monitoring. Key principles: (1) **IV loop diuretics** (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide) are first-line for acute congestion — they promote rapid natriuresis and diuresis, reducing pulmonary edema and symptom burden. (2) **Monitor closely**: urine output (target > 0.5-1 mL/kg/hr), daily weights (target 0.5-1 kg/day net loss), serum electrolytes (K+, Na+, Mg2+), and renal function (Cr). (3) **Avoid over-diuresis**: HFpEF patients have a **stiff, non-compliant ventricle** that is highly **preload-dependent**. Excessive volume removal drops filling pressures below the steep portion of the Frank-Starling curve, causing **hypotension, low cardiac output, and prerenal AKI**. (Ref: 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old male named Harold with stable, compensated HFpEF (LVEF 58%) and NYHA Class II symptoms asks about exercise. Which of the following is the most accurate evidence-based recommendation?', '["Exercise provides no measurable benefit in HFpEF according to all published randomized controlled trials", "Exercise should be deferred until the ejection fraction drops below 40%, at which point cardiac rehabilitation becomes beneficial", "Only isometric (static) strength training is recommended in HFpEF; aerobic exercise is harmful", "Supervised moderate-intensity aerobic exercise training is strongly recommended in stable HFpEF; the Ex-DHF trial and multiple meta-analyses demonstrated that structured exercise programs improve peak VO2 (cardiorespiratory fitness), quality of life, diastolic function (E/e'' ratio), and 6-minute walk distance", "All physical activity is strictly contraindicated in HFpEF because exercise increases cardiac filling pressures and invariably causes acute decompensation"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Exercise training** is one of the **most effective interventions** for improving functional capacity in HFpEF. The **Ex-DHF (Exercise Training in Diastolic Heart Failure) trial** and subsequent **meta-analyses** demonstrated that supervised moderate-intensity aerobic exercise: (1) **Improves peak VO2** (cardiorespiratory fitness) by 2-3 mL/kg/min (clinically meaningful), (2) **Improves quality of life** (KCCQ and SF-36 scores), (3) **Improves diastolic function** (reduced E/e'' ratio), (4) **Improves 6-minute walk distance**, (5) **Reduces left atrial volume**. The 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guidelines give exercise a **Class I recommendation** for all stable HF patients (including HFpEF). Mechanism: exercise training improves peripheral oxygen extraction, endothelial function, skeletal muscle efficiency, and vascular compliance. (Ref: Ex-DHF Trial; 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; Pandey A et al., JACC 2017)'),
