    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old female named Edith with stable, compensated HFpEF (LVEF 68%) and NYHA Class II symptoms asks about exercise. Which of the following is the most accurate evidence-based recommendation?', '["Exercise provides no measurable benefit in HFpEF according to all published randomized controlled trials", "Supervised moderate-intensity aerobic exercise training is strongly recommended in stable HFpEF; the Ex-DHF trial and multiple meta-analyses demonstrated that structured exercise programs improve peak VO2 (cardiorespiratory fitness), quality of life, diastolic function (E/e'' ratio), and 6-minute walk distance", "Exercise should be deferred until the ejection fraction drops below 40%, at which point cardiac rehabilitation becomes beneficial", "Only isometric (static) strength training is recommended in HFpEF; aerobic exercise is harmful", "All physical activity is strictly contraindicated in HFpEF because exercise increases cardiac filling pressures and invariably causes acute decompensation"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Exercise training** is one of the **most effective interventions** for improving functional capacity in HFpEF. The **Ex-DHF (Exercise Training in Diastolic Heart Failure) trial** and subsequent **meta-analyses** demonstrated that supervised moderate-intensity aerobic exercise: (1) **Improves peak VO2** (cardiorespiratory fitness) by 2-3 mL/kg/min (clinically meaningful), (2) **Improves quality of life** (KCCQ and SF-36 scores), (3) **Improves diastolic function** (reduced E/e'' ratio), (4) **Improves 6-minute walk distance**, (5) **Reduces left atrial volume**. The 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guidelines give exercise a **Class I recommendation** for all stable HF patients (including HFpEF). Mechanism: exercise training improves peripheral oxygen extraction, endothelial function, skeletal muscle efficiency, and vascular compliance. (Ref: Ex-DHF Trial; 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; Pandey A et al., JACC 2017)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female named Elizabeth with HFpEF (LVEF 65%) develops new-onset atrial fibrillation with a ventricular rate of 128 bpm. She becomes acutely dyspneic with worsening edema. Why does atrial fibrillation cause disproportionate hemodynamic deterioration in patients with HFpEF compared to those with normal diastolic function?', '["In HFpEF, the stiff, non-compliant ventricle depends heavily on the atrial contraction (''atrial kick'') for adequate diastolic filling (contributing up to 25-40% of stroke volume); loss of the atrial kick in AF, combined with tachycardia-induced shortening of diastolic filling time, causes a precipitous drop in cardiac output and rapid volume overload", "Atrial fibrillation in HFpEF is always hemodynamically benign and requires no rate control or rhythm management", "Atrial fibrillation improves diastolic filling in HFpEF by increasing the atrial contractile force through fibrillatory waves", "The hemodynamic deterioration is caused exclusively by the loss of AV nodal conduction and has no relationship to ventricular stiffness", "Atrial fibrillation causes identical hemodynamic effects in all patients regardless of ventricular compliance or diastolic function"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Atrial fibrillation (AF)** has a **disproportionately severe hemodynamic impact** in HFpEF compared to patients with normal diastolic function. In HFpEF, the **stiff, non-compliant left ventricle** has impaired passive early diastolic filling and relies heavily on the **atrial contraction (atrial kick)** to fill the ventricle during late diastole. The atrial kick contributes **25-40% of LV stroke volume** in HFpEF (vs. only 15-20% in normal hearts). When AF develops: (1) **Loss of atrial kick** → precipitous reduction in stroke volume, (2) **Tachycardia** → shortened diastolic filling time → further reduction in LV filling, (3) **Elevated LA pressure** → acute pulmonary congestion. Management priorities: (1) **Rate control** (beta-blocker or non-DHP CCB), (2) **Consider rhythm control** (cardioversion) for acute hemodynamic compromise. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 38; Zile MR, Circulation 2004; 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old male named Donald with HFpEF (LVEF 68%) develops new-onset atrial fibrillation with a ventricular rate of 128 bpm. He becomes acutely dyspneic with worsening edema. Why does atrial fibrillation cause disproportionate hemodynamic deterioration in patients with HFpEF compared to those with normal diastolic function?', '["Atrial fibrillation improves diastolic filling in HFpEF by increasing the atrial contractile force through fibrillatory waves", "In HFpEF, the stiff, non-compliant ventricle depends heavily on the atrial contraction (''atrial kick'') for adequate diastolic filling (contributing up to 25-40% of stroke volume); loss of the atrial kick in AF, combined with tachycardia-induced shortening of diastolic filling time, causes a precipitous drop in cardiac output and rapid volume overload", "The hemodynamic deterioration is caused exclusively by the loss of AV nodal conduction and has no relationship to ventricular stiffness", "Atrial fibrillation causes identical hemodynamic effects in all patients regardless of ventricular compliance or diastolic function", "Atrial fibrillation in HFpEF is always hemodynamically benign and requires no rate control or rhythm management"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Atrial fibrillation (AF)** has a **disproportionately severe hemodynamic impact** in HFpEF compared to patients with normal diastolic function. In HFpEF, the **stiff, non-compliant left ventricle** has impaired passive early diastolic filling and relies heavily on the **atrial contraction (atrial kick)** to fill the ventricle during late diastole. The atrial kick contributes **25-40% of LV stroke volume** in HFpEF (vs. only 15-20% in normal hearts). When AF develops: (1) **Loss of atrial kick** → precipitous reduction in stroke volume, (2) **Tachycardia** → shortened diastolic filling time → further reduction in LV filling, (3) **Elevated LA pressure** → acute pulmonary congestion. Management priorities: (1) **Rate control** (beta-blocker or non-D