    (v_topic_id, 'A 96-year-old male named Thomas with HFpEF (LVEF 55%) develops new-onset atrial fibrillation with a ventricular rate of 128 bpm. He becomes acutely dyspneic with worsening edema. Why does atrial fibrillation cause disproportionate hemodynamic deterioration in patients with HFpEF compared to those with normal diastolic function?', '["Atrial fibrillation in HFpEF is always hemodynamically benign and requires no rate control or rhythm management", "Atrial fibrillation improves diastolic filling in HFpEF by increasing the atrial contractile force through fibrillatory waves", "Atrial fibrillation causes identical hemodynamic effects in all patients regardless of ventricular compliance or diastolic function", "In HFpEF, the stiff, non-compliant ventricle depends heavily on the atrial contraction (''atrial kick'') for adequate diastolic filling (contributing up to 25-40% of stroke volume); loss of the atrial kick in AF, combined with tachycardia-induced shortening of diastolic filling time, causes a precipitous drop in cardiac output and rapid volume overload", "The hemodynamic deterioration is caused exclusively by the loss of AV nodal conduction and has no relationship to ventricular stiffness"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Atrial fibrillation (AF)** has a **disproportionately severe hemodynamic impact** in HFpEF compared to patients with normal diastolic function. In HFpEF, the **stiff, non-compliant left ventricle** has impaired passive early diastolic filling and relies heavily on the **atrial contraction (atrial kick)** to fill the ventricle during late diastole. The atrial kick contributes **25-40% of LV stroke volume** in HFpEF (vs. only 15-20% in normal hearts). When AF develops: (1) **Loss of atrial kick** → precipitous reduction in stroke volume, (2) **Tachycardia** → shortened diastolic filling time → further reduction in LV filling, (3) **Elevated LA pressure** → acute pulmonary congestion. Management priorities: (1) **Rate control** (beta-blocker or non-D