 CCB), (2) **Consider rhythm control** (cardioversion) for acute hemodynamic compromise. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 38; Zile MR, Circulation 2004; 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female named Hazel presents with dyspnea and edema. Echocardiography is performed. Which of the following comparisons between HFpEF and HFrEF is most accurate?', '["HFpEF is defined by LVEF <= 30% with severe systolic dysfunction, while HFrEF is defined by LVEF > 65% with diastolic dysfunction", "The distinction between HFpEF and HFrEF is purely academic and has no implications for treatment or prognosis", "HFpEF exclusively affects young males with ischemic cardiomyopathy, while HFrEF exclusively affects older females with hypertension", "HFpEF and HFrEF are identical conditions with the same pathophysiology, demographics, and treatment, distinguished only by the arbitrary cutoff of 50% EF", "HFpEF features LVEF >= 50%, concentric LV hypertrophy, normal or small LV cavity, impaired diastolic relaxation, elevated E/e'' ratio, and is typically associated with older age, female sex, HTN, obesity, and AF; HFrEF features LVEF <= 40%, eccentric LV dilation, thin LV walls, reduced systolic contractility, and is typically associated with prior MI, ischemic cardiomyopathy, or dilated cardiomyopathy"]'::jsonb, 4, '**HFpEF vs. HFrEF** are distinct clinical entities:

| Feature | HFpEF | HFrEF |
|---|---|---|
| LVEF | >= 50% | <= 40% |
| LV geometry | Concentric hypertrophy, normal/small cavity | Eccentric dilation, thin walls |
| Primary dysfunction | Diastolic (impaired relaxation/increased stiffness) | Systolic (reduced contractility) |
| Demographics | Older, female, obese, hypertensive | Younger, male, post-MI, ischemic |
| BNP levels | Lower (less wall stress) | Higher (more wall stress) |
| Proven therapies | SGLT2i, diuretics, exercise | ACEi/ARB/ARNI, BB, MRA, SGLT2i |

(Ref: 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; Borlaug BA, JACC 2014)'),
