    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old male named John with HFpEF (LVEF 62%) is being considered for sacubitril/valsartan (angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor, ARNI). Which of the following best summarizes the evidence from the PARAGON-HF trial regarding ARNI therapy in HFpEF?', '["Sacubitril/valsartan is absolutely contraindicated in HFpEF because neprilysin inhibition worsens diastolic stiffness", "PARAGON-HF was terminated early due to excess mortality in the sacubitril/valsartan arm", "The PARAGON-HF trial showed that sacubitril/valsartan did NOT significantly reduce the primary composite endpoint of total HF hospitalizations and cardiovascular death compared to valsartan alone in the overall HFpEF population (LVEF >= 45%); however, prespecified subgroup analyses suggested potential benefit in patients with lower LVEF (45-57%) and in women, leading to FDA expansion of the indication to include HF with LVEF ''below normal''", "PARAGON-HF tested sacubitril/valsartan exclusively in HFrEF patients and provided no data on HFpEF", "PARAGON-HF demonstrated a 50% reduction in all-cause mortality with sacubitril/valsartan in all HFpEF patients, establishing it as first-line therapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **PARAGON-HF (Prospective Comparison of ARNI with ARB Global Outcomes in HFpEF) trial** was a landmark RCT (4,822 patients, LVEF >= 45%) comparing **sacubitril/valsartan vs. valsartan alone**. Results: (1) The primary composite endpoint (total HF hospitalizations + CV death) was **NOT significantly reduced** (rate ratio 0.87, p = 0.06 — just missed statistical significance). (2) **Subgroup analyses** suggested benefit in: (a) **Patients with lower LVEF (45-57%)** (the mildly reduced range), and (b) **Women**. (3) Based on these findings and the PARADIGM-HF data in HFrEF, the FDA expanded the sacubitril/valsartan indication to include HF patients with **LVEF ''below normal''**. (4) The 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guidelines give sacubitril/valsartan a **Class 2b recommendation** in selected HFpEF patients. (Ref: PARAGON-HF Trial, NEJM 2019; 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38)'),
